# November 20, 2026: Nevada, first scatter across the state (it had one place, Rhyolite). Ground taken with
# tools/choose.py --thinnest (nv). Forts and stage stations on the Carson River, the Comstock at Virginia City, the
# silver and gold camps of the middle of the state (Berlin, Belmont, Austin, Eureka, Tonopah, Goldfield), the copper
# railway at Ely, the Ward ovens, Pioche and Delamar in Lincoln County, Lovelock, and Las Vegas and drowned St. Thomas.
# Sources read in this session: Wikipedia (tools/wiki_raw.py), Nevada State Parks pages (parks.nv.gov, read with
# tools/page.py), the National Park Service's St. Thomas trail page, the Nevada State Historic Preservation Office's
# marker page for Stokes Castle, the museums' own pages (fourthwardschool.org, elyrailroadmuseum.org,
# tonopahminingpark.com, mizpahhotel.net), a BLM announcement on Delamar, and Travel Nevada (the state tourism office)
# where the managing body's own page would not open (Eureka County's page answered 403; nnry.com answered 403).
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'nv_clty','label':'Nevada: forts, camps and courthouses across the state','added':'2026-11-20 01:18'}

NVSP_FC=['Nevada State Parks: Fort Churchill','https://parks.nv.gov/parks/fort-churchill']
TN='Travel Nevada (state tourism office)'

PLACES=[
N('fort-churchill-nv','Fort Churchill','Silver Springs','nv','fort','ruins',39.29255,-119.27091,'site','public','walk','','1860–1869',
  'An Army post begun on July 20, 1860, after the Pyramid Lake War, beside the Carson River near Williams Station, and finished in 1861. It guarded settlers and the Pony Express and Central Overland mail route, and in the Civil War it was a supply depot for the Union Army. When the fort was given up and taken apart, Samuel Buckland bought its buildings and used the lumber for his station down the river. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1961 and a state park in 1957.',
  'The adobe ruins of the fort, held in arrested decay with help from the National Park Service. A self-guided trail with signs goes round the ruins and names each building. The Civilian Conservation Corps built the visitor centre, which has exhibits. The park asks visitors not to climb on or go into the ruins, for safety.',
  'An adobe Civil War fort that a stage-station keeper bought for lumber.',
  'Fort Churchill State Historic Park (Nevada State Parks), on U.S. 95 Alternate 8 miles south of U.S. 50 and south of Silver Springs. Open every day of the year; day use $5 a vehicle ($10 for vehicles from outside Nevada), per the park\'s page. Dogs on a leash of six feet or less; vehicles on the park roads and lots only. Parking lot on Fort Churchill Road by the ruins.',
  [W('Fort Churchill State Historic Park'),NVSP_FC],brick='no',hours='Every day of the year',th=[39.29410,-119.27150],thn='Fort Churchill Road lot'),
N('buckland-station-nv','Buckland Station','Silver Springs','nv','hotel','standing',39.29439,-119.25146,'exact','open','drive','stations','c. 1870',
  'Samuel Buckland settled on the Carson River in 1859 and kept a stage station, a Pony Express stop and an early bridge here. About 1870 he built the present two-storey station and hotel, in a plain Greek Revival style, mostly from lumber salvaged when Fort Churchill was taken apart. The state bought it in the 1990s and added it to the Fort Churchill park in 1997; it was listed on the National Register the same year.',
  'The two-storey frame building of about 1870. The ground floor is open as a small museum on a self-guided tour (park page). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'A stage hotel built out of a dismantled fort.',
  'Part of Fort Churchill State Historic Park (Nevada State Parks), on U.S. 95 Alternate about two miles east of the fort. Museum $1 a person; the park\'s day-use fee also applies. Restrooms and picnic tables behind the building. Hours from the park\'s page.',
  [W('Buckland Station'),['Nevada State Parks: Buckland Station','https://parks.nv.gov/parks/buckland-station']],brick='no',hours='Museum Saturday and Sunday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.; Memorial Day to Labor Day also Thursday and Friday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('pipers-opera-house-nv','Piper\'s Opera House','Virginia City','nv','civic','standing',39.31109,-119.65042,'exact','open','walk','','1885',
  'John Piper, a German immigrant who came with the Comstock rush in 1860, built this opera house in 1885 to replace his 1878 house, which had burned; that one in turn replaced the first, lost in the Great Fire of 1875. Mark Twain spoke from the first Piper\'s stage in 1866, Edwin Booth played Shakespeare here, and the boxer Gentleman Jim Corbett trained in the house in 1897. Travel Nevada says it was condemned in the 1920s and stood empty for nearly 20 years before reopening as a museum and, later, a working opera house.',
  'The 1885 opera house in the Virginia City historic district. Travel Nevada describes a self-guided tour of 20 to 30 minutes through the hall. Interior fabric not checked.',
  'The stage Mark Twain spoke from, rebuilt twice after fires.',
  'A privately run performance venue in Virginia City. Hours not checked with the house itself (its own site was not found): Travel Nevada says self-guided tours run daily from April to October at $3 a person, and the Corner Bar keeps longer hours. Park on the streets of Virginia City (not checked on the ground).',
  [W('Piper\'s Opera House'),[TN+': Piper\'s Opera House','https://travelnevada.com/performing-arts/pipers-opera-house/']],conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('fourth-ward-school-nv','Fourth Ward School','Virginia City','nv','civic','standing',39.30314,-119.65227,'exact','open','walk','','1876–1936',
  'A four-storey Second Empire school with a mansard roof, designed in 1876 by C. M. Bennett for more than 1,000 pupils, with indoor flush toilets, gas lighting and central heat. It graduated its last class in 1936, when pupils moved to a new school built by the Works Progress Administration, and stood shut until 1986, when it opened as a museum. Wikipedia says it is the last school of its design built of wood still standing in the country.',
  'The wooden school at 537 South C Street, with a restored classroom and exhibits on the Comstock, Mark Twain and mining (Wikipedia; museum page). Other interior fabric not checked.',
  'The Comstock\'s four-storey wooden schoolhouse, last of its kind.',
  'Historic Fourth Ward School Museum, run by its own nonprofit. Adults $9, children 6 to 12 $6 (museum page). Winter hours depend on the weather: in snow, call the museum before going. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Fourth Ward School (Virginia City, Nevada)'),['Fourth Ward School Museum: visit','https://fourthwardschool.org/visit/']],brick='no',hours='May 1 to October 31 daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; winter Friday to Monday 11 a.m. to 3 p.m., weather permitting'),
N('berlin-nv','Berlin and its mill','Berlin','nv','town','standing',38.88105,-117.60818,'exact','public','walk','','1896–1911',
  'A gold camp that sprang up in 1896 on the western slope of the Shoshone Mountains. The Berlin Mine gave up 42,000 troy ounces of gold, all from tunnels, and was unprofitable by 1911; the town was empty soon after. It is now kept in Berlin–Ichthyosaur State Park, which also protects fossils of the ichthyosaur Shonisaurus popularis found here from 1928.',
  'The ore mill still stands with its stamps and mercury tables, and so do houses, a blacksmith shop, a stage-coach shop and stable, a machine shop and the assay office; some can be entered and others seen through the windows. There are headframes on some of the shafts. The townsite is a self-guided tour.',
  'A gold camp kept just as it stopped, beside a bed of giant marine reptiles.',
  'Berlin–Ichthyosaur State Park (Nevada State Parks), 23 miles east of Gabbs at about 7,000 feet. Open every day of the year; day use $5 a vehicle ($10 for vehicles from outside Nevada). Fossil House tours $5 for adults (2026: 10 a.m. and 2 p.m., weekends from April 18 and daily from Memorial Day to Labor Day, weekends again to November 1). Drive only on established roads. Lot by the mill.',
  [W('Berlin–Ichthyosaur State Park'),['Nevada State Parks: Berlin–Ichthyosaur','https://parks.nv.gov/parks/berlin-ichthyosaur']],brick='?',hours='Park open every day of the year',th=[38.88195,-117.60915],thn='Berlin townsite lot'),
N('belmont-courthouse-nv','Belmont Courthouse','Belmont','nv','civic','standing',38.59642,-116.87593,'exact','open','walk','','1876–1905',
  'Belmont grew from a silver strike in 1865 and became the seat of Nye County in 1867. Its courthouse, built in 1876 (Travel Nevada says the county was granted $3,400 for it in 1875), served as the county\'s seat of government until 1905, when the seat moved to Tonopah. The state park system had it from 1974 and handed it to Nye County in 2012.',
  'The partially restored courthouse, on the south side of the ghost town. In the town also stand the Cosmopolitan Saloon, the Monitor-Belmont Mill with its tall chimney, the combination mill and miners\' cabins (Wikipedia; Travel Nevada). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'A county courthouse left behind when the county moved to Tonopah.',
  'Owned by Nye County and cared for by the Friends of the Belmont Courthouse, which gives free tours by appointment (donations welcome) by telephone through its listing, per Travel Nevada; the Friends\' own page was not read. Belmont is about 45 miles north-east of Tonopah; few services, with picnic tables and toilets in the town. Some buildings in the town are private businesses or homes. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Belmont Courthouse State Historic Park'),W('Belmont, Nevada'),[TN+': Belmont Courthouse','https://travelnevada.com/historical-interests/belmont-courthouse/']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('stokes-castle-nv','Stokes Castle','Austin','nv','landmark','standing',39.49356,-117.07991,'exact','view','walk','','1896–1897',
  'A three-storey stone tower that Anson Phelps Stokes, a mine developer, railroad man and banker, built in 1896 and 1897 as a summer home for his sons, after a medieval tower he had admired near Rome. The family spent about two months in it in 1897 and a little time in 1898, then sold the mine, the mill and the tower and never came back; the State Historic Preservation Office says it has stood empty since, with one possible exception. It was listed on the National Register in 2003.',
  'A tower of hand-hewn local granite raised with a hand winch and held with rock wedging and clay mortar. It had a fireplace on each floor, balconies on the second and third, and a battlemented roof terrace. State Historical Marker No. 59.',
  'A Roman tower on a Nevada hillside, lived in for two months.',
  'Wikipedia says it is now operated by the Austin Historical Society; whether it opens inside was not found, so see it from outside. On the hill just west of Austin, off U.S. 50; the road up and parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Stokes Castle'),['Nevada SHPO: Stokes Castle marker','https://shpo.nv.gov/nevadas-historical-markers/historical-markers/stokes-castle']],brick='no'),
N('eureka-opera-house-nv','Eureka Opera House','Eureka','nv','civic','standing',39.51224,-115.96047,'exact','open','walk','','1880',
  'Built in 1880 on the site of an Odd Fellows hall that burned in August 1879, and the centre of town life through the mining years. It showed films from 1915 as the Eureka Theater and closed as a picture house in 1958. After three decades shut it was restored in 1993 and is now the county\'s convention and arts centre.',
  'The 1880 hall in the middle of Eureka\'s historic downtown. Travel Nevada names the original 1924 hand-painted stage curtain and a collection of signed photographs of performers in the basement, shown on a self-guided tour. Other interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by Eureka County. Its page answered 403 and was not read. Travel Nevada says it is open Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., sometimes closed at lunch, and free to tour on days without events. On Main Street (U.S. 50) in Eureka; parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Eureka Opera House'),[TN+': Eureka Opera House','https://travelnevada.com/arts-culture/eureka-opera-house/'],['Eureka County: Opera House (not read)','https://eurekacountynv.gov/recreation-culture-in-eureka-county/opera-house']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('ward-charcoal-ovens-nv','Ward Charcoal Ovens','Ely','nv','industry','standing',39.03705,-114.84731,'exact','public','walk','','1876–1879',
  'Six beehive ovens that burned local timber into charcoal for the smelters of Ward, a silver town of about 1,500 founded in 1876 two miles to the north. They worked from 1876 to 1879. They were built of welded tuff by Swiss Italian masons who specialised in such ovens. The land was private until 1956, when the state park commission took a permit to protect them.',
  'Six ovens, each 30 feet high and 27 feet across, with walls 2 feet thick at the base; Wikipedia calls them the best preserved in Nevada. The ovens are open to walk into (park page). Of Ward itself only the smelter, mill foundations and the cemetery are left.',
  'Six stone beehives that fed a silver town\'s smelters for three years.',
  'Ward Charcoal Ovens State Historic Park (Nevada State Parks), about 20 miles south of Ely. Open every day of the year; day use $5 a vehicle ($10 for vehicles from outside Nevada). Dogs on a leash of six feet or less. The mapped lot is beside the ovens.',
  [W('Ward Charcoal Ovens State Historic Park'),['Nevada State Parks: Ward Charcoal Ovens','https://parks.nv.gov/parks/ward-charcoal-ovens']],brick='no',hours='Every day of the year',th=[39.03740,-114.84615],thn='Ward Charcoal Ovens lot'),
N('east-ely-yards-nv','Nevada Northern Railway East Ely yards and depot','Ely','nv','rail','standing',39.25917,-114.86833,'site','open','walk','','1906–1983',
  'The yards and shops of the Nevada Northern Railway, built in 1905 and 1906 to carry copper from the mines round Ely about 140 miles north to the transcontinental lines. The yard carried passengers and freight until 1983, when Kennecott, the owner, gave it to a local nonprofit with all the company\'s records. It became a National Historic Landmark district in 2006, cited as one of the best preserved early 20th-century rail yards in the country. The State of Nevada owns it and a foundation runs it as a working museum.',
  'Original steam locomotives (Nos. 81 and 93 run on scheduled trains; No. 40, the state locomotive, was out for overhaul), wooden box cars, ore cars and passenger cars from the 1910s, and what Wikipedia calls the oldest working tank car and coal-fired steam crane on any American railroad. The state railroad museum is upstairs in the restored depot, with the railway\'s ticket office below.',
  'A whole copper-railway yard handed over with its paperwork.',
  'Owned by the State of Nevada. The Nevada State Railroad Museum, East Ely, is at 1100 Avenue A, upstairs in the depot, entered next to the railway\'s gift shop (museum page); it closes on Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day (Travel Nevada). The Nevada Northern Railway\'s own site (train rides and yard tours) answered 403 and was not read. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Nevada Northern Railway Museum'),W('Nevada Northern Railway'),['East Ely Railroad Depot Museum: hours','https://www.elyrailroadmuseum.org/location-hours-admission/'],[TN+': Nevada State Railroad Museum, East Ely','https://travelnevada.com/museums/east-ely-railroad-depot-museum/']],brick='?',hours='Depot museum Monday to Saturday 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., Sunday 8 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('million-dollar-courthouse-nv','Million Dollar Courthouse','Pioche','nv','civic','standing',37.93178,-114.45275,'exact','open','walk','','1872',
  'The Lincoln County courthouse, built in 1872 in Pioche, a silver town with a name for being one of the roughest in the West. The budget was $26,000; discounted bonds, overruns, kickbacks and interest ran the debt to $670,000 by the end of the century and the total near $1 million, per Travel Nevada. Next door stands the Mountain View Hotel of 1895.',
  'The 1872 courthouse on Lacour Street, open for free tours. Interior fabric not checked.',
  'A $26,000 courthouse that cost nearly a million.',
  'Hours not checked with the county: Travel Nevada says it is open daily 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. and tours are free, with donations asked. Pioche is off U.S. 93 on State Routes 321 and 322. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Pioche, Nevada'),[TN+': Million Dollar Courthouse','https://travelnevada.com/museums/million-dollar-courthouse/']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('delamar-nv','Delamar','Caliente','nv','town','ruins',37.45806,-114.77,'area','view','drive','burned','1894–1909',
  'Prospectors found gold here in 1889 and a camp called Ferguson grew up. Captain Joseph R. De Lamar bought the main mines in 1894 and renamed it. From 1895 to 1900 it was Nevada\'s chief ore producer, with more than 3,000 people by 1897, a hospital, an opera house and churches; water was piped 12 miles and supplies came 150 miles by mule team. Fire nearly wiped the town out in 1900, and the mine shut in 1909. Its nickname, The Widowmaker, is in Wikipedia.',
  'Many stone ruins of buildings made of native rock, some nearly whole, mill foundations, two cemeteries and mill sites (Wikipedia; Travel Nevada).',
  'The Widowmaker: a stone-built gold town in the desert.',
  'On a dirt road about 30 minutes off U.S. 93, nearest Caliente. Travel Nevada says the Delamar Mine is private property; the BLM has closed 47 abandoned mine openings round the town. Who owns the townsite itself was not confirmed, so this entry is marked look-from-the-road. Keep out of shafts and adits. The federal land map puts the townsite coordinate on BLM land (Caliente Field Office). Pin from Wikipedia\'s town coordinate, not the ruins themselves.',
  [W('Delamar, Nevada'),[TN+': Delamar Ghost Town','https://travelnevada.com/ghost-town/delamar-ghost-town/'],['BLM: Delamar abandoned mine closures','https://www.blm.gov/announcement/blm-approves-permanent-closure-abandoned-mine-lands-hazards-lincoln-county-nevada']],brick='no'),
N('goldfield-hotel-nv','Goldfield Hotel','Goldfield','nv','hotel','standing',37.70805,-117.23559,'exact','view','walk','','1907–1908',
  'A four-storey Classical Revival hotel by the Reno architects Curtis and Holesworth, built in 1907 and 1908 on the site of two earlier Goldfield Hotels that had burned. It cost $300,000 to $400,000 and was said to be the finest hotel in Nevada; it had 150 rooms. A developer spent $4 million in the 1980s trying to reopen it before going bankrupt, and later owners\' plans to open it have not come to pass. It is a contributing building in the Goldfield Historic District (1982).',
  'A U-shaped hotel: grey Rocklin granite on the ground floor and red brick above, with a white cornice, on the corner of Crook and Columbia Avenues.',
  'Nevada\'s grandest hotel of 1908, shut for decades.',
  'Privately owned and closed; see it from U.S. 95 and Columbia Avenue in Goldfield.',
  [W('Goldfield Hotel')],brick='yes'),
N('mizpah-hotel-nv','Mizpah Hotel','Tonopah','nv','hotel','standing',38.06834,-117.23069,'exact','open','walk','','1907',
  'A five-storey hotel named after the Mizpah Mine and financed by George Wingfield, George S. Nixon and partners. With the Belvada Building it was the tallest building in Nevada until 1927. It was shut from 1999 until 2011, when Fred and Nancy Cline bought it, restored it and reopened it.',
  'Reinforced concrete faced with stone on the front and brick on the sides and rear (Wikipedia). A working hotel with a Jack Dempsey Room and a Wyatt Earp Bar, named for legends Wikipedia says are not true. Interior fabric not checked.',
  'The tallest building in Nevada for twenty years, still taking guests.',
  'A working hotel in the middle of Tonopah; the public rooms are for guests and diners. Hours not found on its page. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Mizpah Hotel'),['Mizpah Hotel','https://www.mizpahhotel.net/']],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('tonopah-mining-park-nv','Tonopah Historic Mining Park','Tonopah','nv','industry','standing',38.069,-117.229,'site','open','walk','','1901–1956',
  'Part of the workings of the Tonopah Mining Company, founded in 1901 after Jim Butler\'s silver strike, and of other Tonopah companies; the company stopped work by 1956. A foundation took the ground over in 1992 as a park for Tonopah\'s mining history.',
  'Four mine sites on 113 acres with much of their original equipment and buildings, a visitor centre and a self-guided walking tour.',
  'Headframes and hoists left where the silver came out.',
  'Run by the Tonopah Historic Mining Park Foundation. Admission to the park and self-guided tour free, guided tours $7 to $12 a person; closed on federal and state holidays (park page). Mapped lot on East McCulloch Avenue.',
  [W('Tonopah Historic Mining Park'),['Tonopah Historic Mining Park','https://www.tonopahminingpark.com/']],brick='?',hours='Daily; summer 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., winter 8 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed federal and state holidays',th=[38.06996,-117.22583],thn='Historic Mining Park parking'),
N('old-las-vegas-mormon-fort-nv','Old Las Vegas Mormon Fort','Las Vegas','nv','fort','ruins',36.18056,-115.13361,'site','open','walk','','1855',
  'Mormon missionaries led by William Bringhurst came on June 14, 1855, and built an adobe fort beside a creek from the Las Vegas Springs, as a halfway stop between Salt Lake City and Los Angeles. Its adobe walls were 14 feet high and 150 feet long. It was given up at the start of the Utah War, became a ranch held by Octavius Gass and then the Stewarts, and passed to the railroad with most of what became downtown Las Vegas. Wikipedia calls it the first permanent building in Las Vegas; the city bought it in 1989.',
  'The remains of the 1855 adobe fort, with a museum and a visitor centre of exhibits and photographs (park page). Which parts of the walls are original was not checked.',
  'The first building in Las Vegas, older than the city by fifty years.',
  'Old Las Vegas Mormon Fort State Historic Park (Nevada State Parks), 500 East Washington Avenue at Las Vegas Boulevard. Museum $3, children 12 and under free. No dogs except service animals; no bicycles in the park. Parking not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Old Las Vegas Mormon Fort State Historic Park'),['Nevada State Parks: Old Las Vegas Mormon Fort','https://parks.nv.gov/parks/old-las-vegas-mormon-fort']],brick='no',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., last admittance 4 p.m.; closed Sunday and Monday'),
N('st-thomas-nv','St. Thomas','Overton','nv','town','water',36.46583,-114.37028,'area','public','hike','drowned','1865–1930s',
  'A town founded by Mormon settlers led by Thomas Smith in 1865 near where the Muddy River meets the Colorado; John Wesley Powell ended his first Colorado River expedition here in 1869. The Mormon settlers left in 1871 when a survey put the town in Nevada, which demanded back taxes in gold. Others took over the farms, and at its peak it held about 500 people. The federal government bought it out and Lake Mead covered it in the 1930s; the ruins came back up as the lake fell in the 2000s. The cemetery was moved to Overton.',
  'Building foundations and smaller traces of the town along an unmaintained loop trail (National Park Service).',
  'A Mormon farm town drowned by Lake Mead and back in the sun.',
  'Lake Mead National Recreation Area (National Park Service), a fee area: keep your park pass in the car. Park at the end of the Old St. Thomas access road, just past mile post 46 on Northshore Road; the trail is about 2.5 miles round trip, unmaintained, starts downhill on loose rock and has no water at the trailhead. Disturbing or removing artifacts is against the law. The pin is the general area from Wikipedia.',
  [W('St. Thomas, Nevada'),['NPS: Hike the St. Thomas trail','https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/hike-the-st-thomas-trail.htm']],brick='?',th=[36.47051,-114.38536],thn='Old St. Thomas Road trailhead'),
N('pershing-county-courthouse-nv','Pershing County Courthouse','Lovelock','nv','civic','standing',40.18084,-118.4765,'exact','view','walk','','1920–1921',
  'Built in 1920 and 1921 for the new Pershing County, which was split from Humboldt County after a quarrel over paying for Humboldt\'s new courthouse. The state\'s supervising architect Frederic J. DeLongchamps designed it, his seventh Nevada courthouse. It cost $99,138.68.',
  'A hexagonal building with curving walls round a circular courtroom under a shallow dome likened to Jefferson\'s library at the University of Virginia, behind a shallow Ionic portico. Brick with stone trim and terra cotta (Wikipedia; the colour of the brick not checked). Interior not checked.',
  'A round courtroom under a dome in a desert county seat.',
  'A working county courthouse in Lovelock; see it from the street. Wikipedia\'s coordinate fell about 4 km south-west of town; the pin is OpenStreetMap\'s building.',
  [W('Pershing County Courthouse')],brick='?'),
]

